    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Cleaning & Shop Tools",
    tags: [
      "best shop vac",
      "wet dry vac",
      "shop vacuum for garage",
      "shop vac hose size",
      "shop vac filter",
      "dust extractor"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          The 7 Best Shop Vacs for Garages, Workshops and Wet Messes
        </h1>
        <QuickAnswer products={PRODUCTS} />
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          A shop vac should match the mess it meets most: sawdust and drywall dust, gravel and leaves in the garage, or water on the basement floor. These seven cover each one.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Shaheer Ali Khan</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Materials &amp; Product Performance Specialist &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          We judge every product on its spec sheet, its label and what owners report over time, not on the marketing. No paid placements, and commission never sets a ranking. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">13 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>Top Picks</span></a>
              <a href="#specs-that-matter" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>Specs That Matter</span></a>
              <a href="#hose" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>Hose Diameter</span></a>
              <a href="#sizing" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>Tank Size</span></a>
              <a href="#filters" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>Filters and Fine Dust</span></a>
              <a href="#extractor" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>Shop Vac or Dust Extractor</span></a>
              <a href="#wet-pickup" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Wet Pickup</span></a>
              <a href="#upgrades" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Setup and Upgrades</span></a>
              <a href="#suction-loss" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>Keeping Suction Strong</span></a>
              <a href="#corded-cordless" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Corded or Cordless</span></a>
              <a href="#cost" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>What to Spend</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">13.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Shop vacs are sold on two numbers that tell you almost nothing, tank gallons and peak horsepower, while the things that decide whether one works for you sit in the fine print: the hose diameter, the airflow, the filter, and how easy it is to empty. A 16-gallon machine with a narrow hose clogs on wood chips that a 9-gallon model with a 2-1/2-inch hose swallows without slowing down. A big vac with a basic paper filter blows fine drywall dust straight back into the room. This guide ranks seven wet/dry vacs by the jobs they suit, then explains how to size, filter, set up and maintain whichever one you choose.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 7 Best Shop Vacs, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Picks are based on manufacturer specifications for airflow, sealed suction, hose diameter, tank capacity and filtration, along with warranty terms, accessory availability and recurring themes in owner feedback. We weighted each machine by how well it suits a realistic garage, workshop or basement job. No hands-on testing is claimed, and no brand paid for placement.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="specs-that-matter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Specs That Matter, and the One That Does Not
          </h2>

          <p>
            Peak horsepower is the figure printed largest on most boxes, and it is close to meaningless. It describes the motor's output in a momentary, no-load condition that never occurs while you vacuum. Two vacs with the same peak HP can perform very differently depending on the fan, the housing and the hose. Treat it as a rough size class at best, and look past it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two numbers tell you more. Airflow, listed in CFM, is how much air the vac moves; high airflow picks up light, bulky debris such as sawdust, leaves and drywall dust. Sealed suction, also called water lift and listed in inches, is how hard the vac can pull against a blocked opening; high water lift helps with heavy debris, wet pickup and long hose runs. Brands that publish both give you a real basis for comparison.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">What to Look For</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2.5 in.</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Hose for chips and debris</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">100+ CFM</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Airflow for general cleanup</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50+ in.</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Water lift for wet pickup</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">9-16 gal</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical garage tank</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those figures are typical guidance for mid-size corded shop vacs, not strict thresholds. Many manufacturers publish CFM measured at the motor rather than at the end of the hose, which overstates what you get in practice. Still, a vac with modest peak HP but strong published airflow and a wide hose will usually outperform one with a bigger HP number and a narrow hose.
          </p>

          <p>
            Beyond performance, check the practical features: a drain port for water, casters that roll over extension cords, on-board hose and cord storage, a blower port, and the availability of filters and bags at a nearby store. A vac with filters that are hard to find becomes a vac with a clogged filter, which is a vac that does not work.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Hose Diameter Decides What It Can Pick Up
          </h2>

          <p>
            Hose diameter is the most practical spec on a shop vac. A 2-1/2-inch hose passes wood chips, small pieces of drywall, screws, leaves and gravel without catching. A 1.875-inch hose works for most dust and small debris but clogs more often on bulky material. A 1.25-inch hose, common on compact vacs, is for cars and small spills and clogs readily on anything bigger than coarse sawdust.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wider hoses move more air at a lower speed, and narrower hoses move less air at a higher speed. That is why a narrow hose feels strong at the nozzle and is good for pulling dust out of crevices and car upholstery, while a wide hose is better for volume. Many woodworkers keep both: a wide hose for floor cleanup and a narrower hose or adapter for connecting to tool dust ports.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Hose</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best For</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Struggles With</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1.25 in.</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Car interiors, small spills, crevices</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Chips, leaves, debris</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1.875 in.</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">General dust, tool ports, light debris</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Large chips, gravel</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2.5 in.</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Garage and workshop floors, renovation debris</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Tight crevices without an adapter</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Hose length matters too. Longer hoses reduce suction at the nozzle, and every bend and adapter costs airflow. Keep the vac close to the work and use the shortest hose that reaches. For reaching far corners of a garage, moving the vac is usually better than adding hose length.
          </p>

          <p>
            Accessory compatibility follows hose diameter. Most brands share common 1.25, 1.875 and 2.5-inch fittings, and adapter kits exist to connect vacs to power tools. Before buying a planer or sander, check its dust port size against your hose. A <a href="/best-benchtop-planer/">benchtop planer</a>, for instance, produces enough chips that only a 2-1/2-inch hose or larger keeps up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Choosing a Tank Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            Bigger is not automatically better. A larger tank means fewer trips to empty it, which matters for water and renovation debris. It also means more weight, more floor space and a vac that is harder to carry up stairs or lift into a truck. For dry dust, the filter clogs long before a large tank fills, so extra gallons do little for workshop cleanup.
          </p>

          <p>
            As a rough guide, 2.5 to 5 gallons suits cars, apartments and small spills. Six to 9 gallons suits a one-car garage, a small workshop or general household use. Twelve to 16 gallons suits a two-car garage, frequent renovation work and water pickup. Above 16 gallons is for contractors and anyone who regularly deals with flooding.
          </p>

          <p>
            Remember that water is heavy, roughly 8.3 pounds per gallon. A 16-gallon tank filled to its usable wet capacity can weigh well over 100 pounds, and the vac shuts off at a float switch before it is completely full. A drain port at the bottom of the tank is essential for any vac you will use on water regularly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Many households end up with two vacs: a mid-size or large corded vac for the garage and shop, and a small one for cars and quick jobs. That combination is often more useful than one large machine, and it costs less than many premium single vacs. The picks above include one in each size class for that reason.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="filters" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Filters, Bags and Fine Dust
          </h2>

          <p>
            The filter decides what goes back into the air. Standard paper cartridge filters catch sawdust, dirt and debris but let fine dust through, and a vac with the wrong filter can blow drywall, concrete or sanding dust all over the room. For those materials, use a fine dust cartridge, a HEPA-rated filter, or a collection bag inside the tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            Collection bags are underrated. They catch most of the fine dust before it reaches the filter, which keeps the filter cleaner, maintains suction longer and makes emptying much less messy. For drywall sanding, concrete grinding and any fine dust, a bag plus a fine dust or HEPA filter is the setup to use.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fine dust from cutting or grinding concrete, stone, brick and mortar contains crystalline silica, which is a serious respiratory hazard. OSHA's silica standard for construction specifies vacuums with HEPA filters for cleanup in many situations, and homeowners doing the same work should follow similar practices. Wear a properly fitted respirator rated for the dust, avoid dry sweeping, and use a HEPA vac. Concrete grinding before coating a garage floor, covered in our <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">garage floor epoxy guide</a>, is a common case.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">Before Wet Pickup</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Take the paper filter out.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Most paper cartridge filters are ruined by water. Remove the dry filter and fit a foam sleeve over the cage before vacuuming liquids, then let everything dry fully before refitting the paper filter.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Clean filters regularly. Tap them out over a trash can outdoors, or use compressed air from the clean side outward while wearing a mask. Replace filters that are torn, permanently caked or damaged by water. A spare filter on the shelf means you can swap and keep working while the dirty one is cleaned and dried.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="extractor" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Shop Vac or Dust Extractor?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A shop vac and a dust extractor look alike but are designed for different jobs. A shop vac is built to pick up a wide range of debris, including water and bulky material, off floors and surfaces. A dust extractor is built to connect to a power tool and capture fine dust as it is produced. Extractors are generally quieter, have better filtration, and often include an auto-start outlet that runs the vac with the tool.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you sand, rout or cut indoors regularly, an extractor is worth its higher price. Capturing dust at the tool keeps the air and surfaces cleaner than cleaning up afterward. It also matters for finish quality, because dust settling on wet finish ruins it. Woodworkers who sand cabinet parts, furniture or trim notice the difference immediately.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your work is mainly garage cleanup, car interiors, water and the occasional sawdust pile, a shop vac is the better tool and much less expensive. Many shop vacs can be connected to tools with adapters, and some offer auto-start outlets. With a fine dust filter and a bag, a good shop vac does a reasonable job on tool dust, especially for occasional use.
          </p>

          <p>
            For tools that make chips rather than dust, such as planers, jointers and thickness sanders, neither a basic vac nor an extractor is ideal for long sessions; a dust collector with a larger hose is the proper tool. A shop vac with a 2-1/2-inch hose and a cyclone separator can handle a small benchtop planer for occasional use. For tool brand choices that affect battery and dust port ecosystems, see <a href="/milwaukee-vs-dewalt/">Milwaukee vs DeWalt</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wet-pickup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Wet Pickup Without the Problems
          </h2>

          <p>
            Before picking up water, remove the paper filter and put on a foam sleeve, or use the filter the manufacturer specifies for wet use. Make sure the float in the cage moves freely; it shuts off airflow when the tank is full so water does not reach the motor. If the vac suddenly sounds higher-pitched and suction drops, the float has closed and the tank needs emptying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electrical safety matters around water. Plug into a GFCI-protected outlet, keep the cord connection off wet floors, and avoid using the vac in standing water deeper than the floor if the motor housing or cord could be submerged. If a basement has flooded deeply or the water reaches outlets or appliances, have the power shut off at the panel and let a professional assess it before you go in.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not vacuum flammable liquids such as gasoline, solvents or paint thinner. The motor can ignite vapors. Do not vacuum hot ashes, embers or burning material, which can smolder in the tank for hours. Do not vacuum toxic materials unless the vac is specifically rated for them. Fine powders like drywall dust also need the right filter, as covered above.
          </p>

          <p>
            After wet use, empty and rinse the tank, wipe out the float cage, and let everything dry with the lid off before storing. A tank left closed with water in it grows mildew and smells for weeks. Rinse the hose too, especially after picking up dirty water, and hang it to drain.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="upgrades" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Setup and Upgrades Worth Making
          </h2>

          <p>
            A cyclone separator is the most effective upgrade for a shop vac used on sawdust and chips. It sits between the hose and the vac, usually on top of a bucket, and spins debris out of the airstream before it reaches the filter. The vac keeps strong suction much longer, the filter stays clean, and you empty a bucket instead of the vac. For anyone using a vac with a planer, miter saw or router table, it is worth adding.
          </p>

          <p>
            An auto-start switch, either built into the vac or added as a separate plug-in device, turns the vac on and off with your power tool. It makes dust collection effortless enough that you will actually use it. Check that the combined current draw of tool and vac does not exceed the circuit, since a 15-amp tool and a vac on the same 15-amp circuit will trip a breaker.
          </p>

          <p>
            Better hoses and nozzles help too. A crush-resistant hose with smooth walls loses less airflow than a ribbed one. A wide floor nozzle with a squeegee helps with water. A crevice tool, a brush and a narrow nozzle cover car interiors. A wall mount or a cart keeps the vac and accessories organized, which matters more than it sounds: a vac that is easy to grab gets used.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a garage that doubles as a car-care space, keeping a shop vac alongside a <a href="/best-pressure-washer/">pressure washer</a> covers most cleaning jobs inside and out. Vacuum the interior and the garage floor, wash the exterior, and use the vac to pick up standing water when you are done.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="suction-loss" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Keeping Suction Strong
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most suction complaints have simple causes. In rough order of likelihood: a clogged filter, a full tank or closed float, a blockage in the hose or nozzle, a loose lid or missing gasket, or a hole in the hose. Check them in that order before assuming the motor is failing. Motors do wear out, but far less often than filters clog.
          </p>

          <p>
            To find a blockage, disconnect the hose and feel the suction at the inlet on the tank. If it is strong there, the problem is in the hose or nozzle. Drop a coin or a small object through the hose to find the clog, or push a broom handle through it gently. If suction at the inlet is weak, the problem is the filter, float, lid seal or motor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fine dust is the most common cause of a clogged filter. A cartridge used on drywall dust can lose most of its airflow in minutes without a bag. Using a bag or a cyclone separator for fine dust avoids most of these problems, and cleaning or replacing the filter promptly keeps suction consistent.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the motor sounds labored, smells hot or shows sparks through the vents, stop and inspect it. Motor brushes wear on universal motors, and some vacs allow brush replacement. Weigh repair costs against the price of a new vac; for budget models, replacement is often more economical.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="corded-cordless" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Corded or Cordless
          </h2>

          <p>
            Corded vacs still win on sustained airflow per dollar. A plug-in motor can run for an hour on a garage cleanout without fading, and it costs a fraction of a battery model with packs included. For a home workshop, a basement or a garage with outlets on every wall, a corded machine is the sensible default and will remain so for the foreseeable future.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery vacs earn their place in three situations. The first is vehicles, where dragging a cord around a car or truck is clumsy and a compact battery vac reaches every seat. The second is job sites and remote corners of a property without power. The third is quick cleanups, where grabbing a cordless vac off a shelf is faster than unwinding a cord, so the mess actually gets dealt with right away.
          </p>

          <p>
            The cost case depends entirely on what you own. Bare cordless vacs from the major tool brands are reasonably priced, but buying high-capacity batteries and a charger separately can double the outlay. If you already run a platform from Milwaukee, DeWalt, Ryobi or Makita, a bare vac is an easy add. If you do not, a corded vac plus a cheap handheld is usually the better buy, and our <a href="/ryobi-tools-review/">Ryobi platform review</a> is a useful starting point if you are picking a system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Runtime on battery vacs varies widely with battery size and how hard the motor works. Expect a large pack to give a useful session for spot cleaning and vehicles rather than a whole-garage cleanout. Wet pickup also drains batteries quickly. Keep a second charged battery on hand if you plan longer jobs, and store packs indoors in cold weather to protect their capacity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            11. What to Spend
          </h2>

          <p>
            At the time of writing, compact 2.5 to 5-gallon vacs run roughly $50 to $100. Mid-size 6 to 9-gallon vacs run roughly $80 to $150. Large 12 to 16-gallon vacs run roughly $120 to $220. Cordless vacs from tool brands run roughly $150 to $350 for the bare tool, more with batteries. Dust extractors run roughly $400 to $900 or more.
          </p>

