        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming Water With the Paper Filter In</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Paper cartridges absorb water, collapse and grow mildew, and a soaked filter blocks airflow so the vac labors. Remove the dry filter before wet pickup and use a foam sleeve or the wet filter the manual specifies. After the job, dry the tank and cage completely before reinstalling the paper filter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sucking Up Drywall Dust Without a Bag</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Drywall and concrete dust pass through basic filters and coat the room, and what stays clogs the filter in minutes. Use a collection bag and a fine dust or HEPA filter for these materials. The bag catches most of the dust, keeps suction steady and makes emptying far less messy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Choosing by Gallons Instead of Hose Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A big tank with a narrow hose clogs on chips and leaves that a smaller vac with a wide hose handles easily. For garage and workshop debris, a 2-1/2-inch hose matters more than extra gallons. Choose the hose first, then pick the smallest tank that suits your water and debris volume.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Vacuuming Hot Ash or Flammable Liquids</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Embers can smolder inside the tank for hours and start a fire after you have put the vac away. Gasoline, solvents and thinner give off vapors the motor can ignite. Let ashes cool fully and use a metal ash vac rated for the job, and clean up flammable liquids with absorbent material instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Cleaning the Filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A filter caked with dust can cut airflow dramatically, and owners often blame the motor. Tap the filter out after dusty jobs, keep a spare to swap in, and replace torn or water-damaged cartridges. A clean filter is the cheapest way to keep a shop vac working like new.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which shop vac is best for a typical garage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most garages and home workshops, a 12 to 14-gallon vac with a 2-1/2-inch hose and a drain port is the right size, and the Ridgid HD1400 NXT fits that description well. If you regularly deal with flooding or renovation debris, the Craftsman 16-gallon is worth the extra size. For cars and small spills, a compact 5 or 6-gallon vac is easier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is peak horsepower a useful way to compare shop vacs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. The peak figure describes momentary motor output with no load, which never happens while vacuuming. Airflow in CFM and sealed suction in inches of water lift are more meaningful, along with hose diameter. Two vacs with the same peak HP rating can perform quite differently depending on the fan, housing and hose design.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What hose diameter should I choose?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For garage and workshop debris, choose a 2-1/2-inch hose, which passes chips, leaves and screws without clogging. A 1.875-inch hose suits general dust and connecting to tools. A 1.25-inch hose is for cars and small jobs. Many owners keep a wide hose for floors and an adapter or second hose for tools and tight spaces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is a HEPA filter actually necessary?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If you clean up drywall dust, concrete or masonry dust, or fine sanding dust, a HEPA-rated filter plus a collection bag is strongly recommended. Concrete and masonry dust contain silica, and OSHA guidance for construction calls for HEPA vacuums in many cleanup tasks. For general debris, sawdust and water, a standard filter is fine.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a shop vac pick up water from a flooded basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, for shallow water. Remove the paper filter, fit a foam sleeve, plug into a GFCI outlet and keep the cord connection dry. Empty the tank through the drain port when the float shuts off airflow. For deep flooding, or water near outlets and appliances, cut power at the panel and call a professional before entering.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What usually causes a sudden drop in suction?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The usual causes, in order, are a clogged filter, a full tank with the float closed, a blockage in the hose or nozzle, a loose lid or missing gasket, and a damaged hose. Check suction at the tank inlet with the hose removed to narrow it down. Motor failure is possible but much less common than a clogged filter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a dust extractor instead of a shop vac?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if you sand, rout or cut indoors regularly and want dust captured at the tool. Extractors are quieter and filter better but cost several times more and use smaller hoses that are not ideal for floor debris. For garage cleanup, water and occasional sawdust, a shop vac with a good filter and a bag is the better value.
              </p>
            </div>

